// F21 -- fields that do not belong to the chosen variant are PARSED AND SILENTLY DISCARDED.
//
// Run:
//   java -cp <runner>/target/classes:$(cat cp.txt) perf.TestableMain model.pure --pmcd-json=out.json
// then inspect out.json.
//
// Three instances of one shape, all accepted without a diagnostic:
//
// 1. `mode: local` alongside `specification:` and `auth:`. The walker treats local mode as an
//    if/else -- when it is present the spec and auth branches are never entered. Both blocks
//    still parse, and the protocol then carries SYNTHESISED placeholders instead:
//        datasourceSpecification -> accountName "legend-local-snowflake-accountName-fx-Db" ...
//        authenticationStrategy  -> "snowflakePublic", privateKeyVaultReference "legend-local-..."
//    The vault reference written below does not appear anywhere in the output. A user who
//    writes credentials here is told nothing; they are replaced.
//
// 2. `mappings:` on a JsonModelConnection. All three model connections share ONE definition
//    rule and the walker extracts only what its own type needs, so the field is dropped
//    entirely -- the protocol has only class, url, element.
//
// 3. `class:` on a ModelChainConnection. Same cause, same silence.
//
// Why it matters beyond tidiness: a round-trip through parse-and-compose does not preserve
// the file. Anything re-emitting a model silently rewrites it, and the author is never told
// which of their lines stopped existing. A rewrite that REJECTS these is stricter than
// legend-engine and would break such files; one that keeps them diverges in the protocol.
// Either way it has to be a deliberate choice, which is why this is worth recording.
###Connection
RelationalDatabaseConnection fx::LocalWithIgnoredCredentials
{
   store: fx::Db;
   type: Snowflake;
   mode: local;
   specification: Static { name: 'ignored'; host: 'ignored'; port: 1; };
   auth: ApiToken { apiToken: 'THIS-VALUE-DISAPPEARS'; };
}

JsonModelConnection fx::JsonWithIgnoredMappings
{
   class: fx::A;
   url: 'model://a';
   mappings: [fx::M1, fx::M2];
}

ModelChainConnection fx::ChainWithIgnoredClass
{
   mappings: [fx::M1];
   class: fx::A;
}
